Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{"action": "final", "output": {
  "research_concepts": [
    "defensive adjustment effect on opponents' second-half performance",
    "halftime performance change (H2-H1 delta)",
    "stability and between-team reliability of a per-game defensive metric",
    "team defensive trait"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "how to measure the reliability of a per-game metric like opponent H2-H1 change",
    "how many games per team are needed for reliable team-level estimates",
    "what confounding factors (opponent quality, game state, home/away, rest) affect the metric",
    "whether a simple split-half design can detect a signal given the noise in basketball scoring"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-085",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "Split-half reliability of connector quality Q_p (teammate-initiated on-off PPP gap) was tested on 102 players. Pearson r=+0.215, p=0.0297; Spearman-Brown 0.355, ICC(player-season)=0.216. 78.4% of variance was within player (half-to-half random). The study concluded that Q_p cannot be treated as a stable scoutable trait because most variance is within-player noise, and the measurement reliability is too low."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-018",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "Ranked playsheet validation across 10 playoff series (665 counter observations) tested whether a defense's play-type counter ranking predicted second-half scoring. Full-rank Spearman rho was not significant (−0.0639, p≈0.1). Only pairwise BEST vs AVOID showed a difference (p=0.004). The study was limited by small sample, leakage risk, and low measurement reliability."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF15",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "Test of opponent PPP on next possessions after a star's 2nd foul found an apparent +0.204 PPP rise, which was entirely driven by free-throw possessions (41.8% vs 9.9% of controls). Excluding free throws, the effect was not significant (lag-1 +0.0486, CI crosses zero). The headline result was an artifact caused by mechanical inclusion of free-throw trips."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-068",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "Possession Control Index appeared to add explanatory power for Net Rating (+0.139 dR²), but the gain came from a leakage term that tracks defensive rating (correlation +0.894). Offensive-control components added only +0.005. The model's predictor leaked future/defensive information, creating a spurious improvement."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-085",
      "what_failed": "Split-half reliability estimate for a per-possession on-off PPP difference metric (connector quality Q_p)",
      "why": "Low split-half correlation (r=0.215), low ICC (0.216); most variance within player, indicating that the metric is too noisy to be a stable player trait. Classified as untestable under current design and data."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-018",
      "what_failed": "Validation of a defensive counter-play ranking using H2-H1 play-type changes in a small set of playoff series",
      "why": "Insufficient sample (665 counter observations, few possessions per counter), leakage from regular season to playoffs, and low measurement reliability prevented a conclusive result."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "A directly analogous per-possession difference metric (on-off PPP gap, computed at the player level) had very low split-half reliability (r=0.215, ICC=0.216) and could not be treated as a stable trait.",
    "Even with a moderate number of observations per player, the half-to-half within-player variance dominated, suggesting that basketball performance differences between halves are highly noisy.",
    "Playoff-sample studies of H2-H1 counter-play effectiveness were limited by small sample and unreliability, implying that using such metrics as a reliable trait requires much larger and cleaner data."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether a team-level defensive H2-H1 impact metric is reliable, as no study directly tested that question.",
    "Whether controlling for opponent strength and context could yield a stable team trait, or if the noise is fundamentally too large even with 120 team-seasons and 82 games each.",
    "Whether a more sophisticated design (e.g., possession-level modelling, regularisation, multi-year shrinkage) could extract a signal."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The low reliability found for a per-player half-based PPP metric strongly suggests that a per-game team-level H2-H1 change will also be very noisy, because the underlying scoring events are even more aggregated and confounded.",
    "A team's true ability to adjust defensively at halftime, if it exists, is likely swamped by game-to-game variability in opponent quality, shot randomness, and game-script effects.",
    "The 120 team-seasons may provide enough cross-sectional data to estimate an ICC, but the within-team sample size (41 games per split-half) is modest; power to detect a non-zero between-team variance is likely insufficient unless the trait is extremely strong.",
    "Artifact risks (e.g., free-throw trips after fouls, garbage-time effects, regression to the mean from halftime score) could create spurious stability that mimics a reliable trait."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "Whether a cleaned version of the metric (removing garbage time, controlling for opponent offensive rating, excluding free throws) would improve reliability enough to detect the trait.",
    "How large the true between-team variance in defensive H2-H1 adjustment really is, and whether it persists across multiple seasons.",
    "Whether the 2025‑26 season data (partial) can be used reliably without introducing lookahead or incomplete-game biases."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "Without the historical evidence, we might have assumed that a team-level metric with 120 team-seasons would be testable. However, the record FAM:SF-APPS-085 showed that a similar half-based difference metric had extremely low reliability even at the player level with many possessions. This, combined with the failed validation in FAM:SF-APPS-018, indicates that the noise floor for H2-H1 metrics is high. As a result, the pre-study assessment now leans strongly towards untestability, and the decision not to proceed is directly informed by the historical findings."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.35,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Per-game H2-H1 scoring differences are inherently noisy; historical evidence shows that analogous per-possession half-based metrics have ICC~0.2, meaning most variance is random."
      },
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "Each team-season provides only 82 games; after splitting, each half contributes ~41 games to the team mean. The effective sample size for a reliability correlation is only 120 team-seasons, and the standard error of the split-half correlation may be too large to detect a modest true ICC."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Opponent offensive strength, game location, rest, halftime score margin, and garbage-time minutes are not controlled for and could create spurious between-team differences that do not reflect true defensive adjustment ability."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "unknown",
    "cited": [
      "FAM:SF-APPS-085",
      "FAM:SF-APPS-018"
    ],
    "summary": "No earlier study directly tested whether a defense's effect on opponents' H2-H1 change is a reliable team trait. The two most relevant study families both highlight reliability challenges in metrics that split performance by half."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Game-level opponent H2-H1 PPP changes are highly variable; previous work found ICC<0.25 for analogous half-based metrics at the player level."
    },
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "120 team-seasons with 41 games per split-half will likely lack power to detect a meaningful between-team variance."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Uncontrolled opponent quality and game context (home/away, rest, garbage time) can introduce systematic biases that inflate or mask a true defensive trait."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Teams that underperform defensively in the first half may naturally regress toward the mean in the second half, creating a spurious pattern of 'adjustment'."
    },
    {
      "code": "MECHANICAL_FLOOR",
      "text": "Scoring per half has floor/ceiling effects (especially in blowouts) that can distort H2-H1 differences."
    },
    {
      "code": "BASE_RATE",
      "text": "If genuine halftime defensive adjustments are rare or small in magnitude, the between-team signal may be negligible relative to noise."
    },
    {
      "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
      "text": "If incomplete 2025‑26 games are used for testing, or if future-game data leaks into the metrics (e.g., using season totals to normalise), stability may be overestimated."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Exploring many operationalisations of H2-H1 change (e.g., points per possession, effective field goal percentage) without correction increases risk of false discovery."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.05,
    "rejected": 0.15,
    "untestable": 0.55,
    "artifact": 0.15,
    "uncertain": 0.10
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "Between-team variance in defensive H2-H1 opponent PPP change, expressed as the intraclass correlation (ICC) of the per-game H2-H1 difference, after adjusting for opponent offensive strength, game location, rest, and garbage-time exclusions.",
    "unit": "Team-season (NBA team in a given regular season)",
    "sample": "All 120 team-seasons from the complete 2022‑23, 2023‑24, and 2024‑25 regular seasons (82 games each), excluding the partial 2025‑26 season to avoid lookahead. Only games where both halves have at least 5 possessions are included; games with OT are split by regulation time only.",
    "power": "For an ICC of 0.10 with 120 team-seasons and 82 games each (split into 41 games per half), the approximate standard error of the split-half correlation r would be around 0.09. Power to detect r=0.30 (i.e., ICC~0.30) would be about 80%; to detect r=0.15 would be below 30%. The test would likely be underpowered for realistic true effect sizes.",
    "controls": "Opponent offensive rating (season-level, excluding the game in question to avoid leakage); home/away indicator; rest days since last game; removal of possessions when the score margin exceeds 20 points in the fourth quarter (garbage time); adjustment for halftime score differential via regression to mitigate regression-to-the-mean artefacts.",
    "falsification": "1. Permute team labels among games to generate a null distribution of the split-half correlation; the observed ICC must exceed the 95th percentile of the null. 2. Test the same design on the third vs fourth quarter instead of first vs second half; no theory predicts a stable team trait there, so any significant ICC would suggest systematic noise rather than a meaningful defensive trait. 3. Repeat the analysis using only the 2023‑24 season (30 team-seasons) to see if the result is driven by a single season.",
    "multiplicity": "Primary analysis uses one pre-specified metric: opponent points per possession (PPP) excluding free throws after non-shooting fouls. A single significance test for the ICC using the null-hypothesis that ICC ≤ 0 is conducted; no adjustment for multiple outcomes.",
    "decision_rule": "If the estimated ICC is ≥ 0.25 and the 95% confidence interval lower bound exceeds 0.05, the trait is deemed 'reliable'; otherwise, the hypothesis is rejected or the test is considered inconclusive. This set of thresholds would require a point estimate well above the noise observed in analogous metrics.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022‑23, 2023‑24, 2024‑25 regular-season per-game play-by-play and box-score data from the NBA stats API, filtered to complete games as described. The 2025‑26 season is excluded to ensure no partial or future data enters training or testing."
  }
}}

